      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), we use the midpoint formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the endpoints \\((6, 12)\\) and \\((0, -6)\\), we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 0}{2}, \\frac{12 + (-6)}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the coordinates:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6}{2}, \\frac{12 - 6}{2} \\right) = \\left( 3, \\frac{6}{2} \\right) = \\left( 3, 3 \\right)\n\\]\n\nThe sum of the coordinates of the midpoint is:\n\n\\[\n3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the coordinates of the midpoint of the segment with endpoints \\((6, 12)\\) and \\((0, -6)\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
