      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), we use the midpoint formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the endpoints \\((6, 12)\\) and \\((0, -6)\\), we substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + 0}{2}, \\frac{12 + (-6)}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate each coordinate separately:\n\n1. For the x-coordinate:\n\\[\n\\frac{6 + 0}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\n2. For the y-coordinate:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 + (-6)}{2} = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the coordinates of the midpoint are \\((3, 3)\\).\n\nTo find the sum of these coordinates, we add them together:\n\n\\[\n3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coordinates of the midpoint is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
