      "sample_index": 5,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of a line segment with given endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), we use the midpoint formula:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\nIn this problem, the endpoints are \\((6, 12)\\) and \\((0, -6)\\). So we will calculate the x-coordinate and y-coordinate of the midpoint separately.\n\nThe x-coordinate of the midpoint is:\n\\[\n\\frac{6 + 0}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\nThe y-coordinate of the midpoint is:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 + (-6)}{2} = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\nSo, the coordinates of the midpoint are \\((3, 3)\\).\n\nThe sum of the coordinates of the midpoint is:\n\\[\n3 + 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this by writing and running the Python code.\n```python\n# Define the endpoints\nx1, y1 = 6, 12\nx2, y2 = 0, -6\n\n# Calculate the midpoint\nmidpoint_x = (x1 + x2) / 2\nmidpoint_y = (y1 + y2) / 2\n\n# Calculate the sum of the coordinates of the midpoint\nsum_of_midpoint_coordinates = midpoint_x + midpoint_y\nprint(sum_of_midpoint_coordinates)\n```\n```output\n6.0\n```\nThe sum of the coordinates of the midpoint of the segment with endpoints \\((6, 12)\\) and \\((0, -6)\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
